{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_s6nuog5w5helvooqt33evqynw34mcw2n4qkggg6ii4es4uvuoydq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_014gkGHEagq3hVbvtNZNxU7K","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Hunt for C/Rust divergences","subagent_type":"general-purpose","run_in_background":false,"prompt":"You are an adversarial divergence hunter for a C-to-Rust translation. Your ONLY job is to FIND INPUTS/CONDITIONS WHERE THE RUST BEHAVES DIFFERENTLY FROM THE C. Report them; do not fix anything.\n\nWorking dir: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wClUkV/translated_rust\n\n- C ground truth: c_src/src/main.c (41 lines: static print_hex, void driver(int), int main(void) that does `int x=0; scanf(\"%d\",&x); driver(x);`)\n- Rust translation: src/driver_impl.rs (all logic), src/lib.rs (#[no_mangle] `driver` and `main` exports), src/main.rs (binary).\n\nHARD RULES:\n- Do NOT modify ANY file under the working dir (no edits to c_src/, src/, tests/, Cargo.toml, *.md). Read-only on the repo.\n- Write scratch files ONLY under \"$TMPDIR\" (already set; use it, never /tmp).\n- Use timeouts on every command (e.g. `timeout 300 ...`). Never run a command longer than 600s.\n- Do not run `cargo test` on the existing suite (it is already known to pass); write your OWN independent probes.\n\nAlready-built artifacts you can use (rebuild into $TMPDIR if you prefer):\n- C executable: c_src/build/driver   (built with cmake; rebuild: `gcc -o $TMPDIR/c_exe c_src/src/main.c`)\n- Rust executable: `timeout 300 cargo build --offline` then target/debug/driver\n- C shared lib: `gcc -shared -fPIC -o $TMPDIR/libc.so c_src/src/main.c`  (exports `driver`, `main`)\n- Rust shared lib: `timeout 300 rustc --edition 2021 --crate-type cdylib -A warnings -o $TMPDIR/librs.so src/lib.rs` (exports `driver`, `main`)\n\nYOUR TASK — try hard to break it. Ideas (do these AND invent your own):\n1. Fuzz the executables: feed thousands of random byte strings on stdin to both binaries and diff stdout+exit status. Cover: random ASCII, random bytes, digit strings of every length 1..40, signs, whitespace mixes (space/tab/nl/vt/ff/cr), leading zeros, huge numbers (magnitudes around 2^31, 2^32, 2^63, 2^64, 10^40), junk tails, inputs with NULs, multi-MB inputs, inputs whose interesting byte lands exactly at offsets 4095/4096/4097/8191/8192/8193, no trailing newline, CRLF, etc. Write a shell or python3 loop; report ANY mismatch with the exact input bytes (hex-escaped) and both outputs.\n2. Fuzz the `driver` export through dlopen in both .so files (write your own small C program in $TMPDIR that dlopens each .so and calls driver with values you choose, or use python3 ctypes) for many int values incl. all boundary/bit-pattern shapes; diff the printed bytes.\n3. Exercise the exported `main` symbol MULTIPLE times in one process (dlopen + call main 2..5 times) with streams like \"1 2 3\", \"12x34\", \"12-34\", \"1\", \"\", \"junk\", and diff. (This is known to be modelled via glibc ungetc/EOF state — try to find shapes where it still differs.)\n4. Weird fds: stdin closed, stdin = directory, stdin = /dev/null, stdin = /dev/zero (careful: infinite; use `timeout 5`), stdout closed, stdout = /dev/full, stdout = pipe with no reader (SIGPIPE), stdout = append-mode file. Compare exit codes and signals.\n5. Environment: LC_ALL/LANG/LC_NUMERIC exotic values; huge argv/env; running under `stdbuf`; stdout to a pty if you can (`script -qec ... /dev/null`).\n6. Anything else that could differ: buffering, partial writes, timing of flush relative to output, return value/exit status, signal disposition, resource limits (`ulimit -f 0`? do not break the machine), etc.\n\nReport format: a numbered list of CONFIRMED divergences, each with (a) exact reproduction command, (b) the input bytes hex-escaped, (c) C output/status, (d) Rust output/status. Then a list of things you probed and found IDENTICAL (brief). If you find no divergence, say so explicitly and list the probe coverage with the number of cases per probe. Be precise and honest; do not speculate about divergences you did not actually reproduce."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4231,"cache_read_input_tokens":178109,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4231,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64daaee6-dfd8-4ebc-87be-dd6361eb86bc","uuid":"6a4c9a8c-7b25-4afe-ac6c-aa9b60a624b5","timestamp":"2026-08-18T09:47:41.319Z"}
