{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"aceb7482d82b1d449","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_014gkGHEagq3hVbvtNZNxU7K","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wClUkV/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-wClUkV-translated-rust/64daaee6-dfd8-4ebc-87be-dd6361eb86bc/tasks/aceb7482d82b1d449.output","summary":"## CONFIRMED DIVERGENCES\n\nAll commands below assume:\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wClUkV/translated_rust\nexport TMPDIR=/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wClUkV/tmp\nP=$TMPDIR/probe          # scratch dir with my probes\nC=$P/c_exe               # gcc -O0 -o $P/c_exe c_src/src/main.c\nR=$PWD/target/debug/driver\n# $P/libc.so  = gcc -shared -fPIC -o $P/libc.so c_src/src/main.c\n# $P/librs.so = rustc --edition 2021 --crate-type cdylib -A warnings -o $P/librs.so src/lib.rs\n```\ngcc 11.5.0, glibc 2.34, rustc 1.94.0, x86-64 Linux.\n\n---\n\n### D1. Shared *seekable* stdin: second invocation reads the wrong data (standalone executable, no dlopen, stdout differs)\n\n(a) `printf '1 2 3' > $P/d1.bin; { $C; $C; } < $P/d1.bin` vs `{ $R; $R; } < $P/d1.bin`\n(b) input `\\x31\\x20\\x32\\x20\\x33` (`\"1 2 3\"`, no newline)\n(c) **C:** `01000000\\n02000000\\n`, status 0\n(d) **Rust:** `01000000\\n00000000\\n`, status 0\n\nRoot cause: glibc's `exit` runs `_IO_cleanup` → `_IO_unbuffer_all` → `_IO_new_file_sync`, which `lseek`s fd 0 back by `_IO_read_ptr - _IO_read_end`. The C program therefore leaves fd 0 positioned exactly one byte past the last character `scanf` consumed (offset 1 here). Rust's `Scanner` reads through `io::stdin().lock()`'s 8 KiB `BufReader` (`src/driver_impl.rs:234`, 1-byte reads at `:102-119`) and never restores the offset, so it swallows the whole file. Systematic fuzz: **526 of 599** shared-seekable-fd cases diverge (`$P/fuzz_seek.py`; full log `$P/seek.out`). Examples of divergent stdout:\n\n| input (hex-escaped) | runs | C stdout | Rust stdout |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `\\x31\\x20\\x32\\x20\\x33` | 3 | `01000000 02000000 03000000` | `01000000 00000000 00000000` |\n| `\\x31\\x0a\\x32\\x0a\\x33\\x0a` | 3 | `01000000 02000000 03000000` | `01000000 00000000 00000000` |\n| `\\x31\\x32\\x2d\\x33\\x34` (`12-34`) | 2 | `0c000000 deffffff` | `0c000000 00000000` |\n| `\\x35\\x20\\x36\\x20\\x37\\x20\\x38\\x20\\x39` | 3 | `05000000 06000000 07000000` | `05000000 00000000 00000000` |\n\nAlso visible with `n=1`: final fd offset is 1 (C) vs 5 (Rust).\n\n### D2. Leftover stdin consumed away from a co-reader on the same fd\n\n(a) `printf '1\\nHELLO\\n' > $P/d2.bin; { $C; cat; } < $P/d2.bin` vs `{ $R; cat; } < $P/d2.bin`\n(b) `\\x31\\x0a\\x48\\x45\\x4c\\x4c\\x4f\\x0a`\n(c) **C:** `01000000\\n\\nHELLO\\n`, status 0\n(d) **Rust:** `01000000\\n`, status 0\n\nSame root cause as D1 (glibc's exit-time seek-back vs Rust's unrestored 8 KiB read-ahead).\n\n### D3. Read-ahead chunk size differs on unseekable stdin: 4096 (glibc) vs 8192 (Rust)\n\n(a) `python3 -c \"import sys;sys.stdout.write('7'+' '*5000+'TAIL')\" > $P/d3.bin`\n   `cat $P/d3.bin | { $C >/dev/null; wc -c; }` vs `cat $P/d3.bin | { $R >/dev/null; wc -c; }`\n(b) `\\x37` + 5000 × `\\x20` + `TAIL` (5005 bytes)\n(c) **C:** `909` (glibc `read`s 4096, leaves the rest in the pipe)\n(d) **Rust:** `0` (Rust `read`s 8192, drains the pipe)\n\nNot seek-related — applies to pipes/FIFOs/sockets too. Requires >4096 bytes queued.\n\n### D4. Huge digit run under a memory limit: C reports an input failure, Rust returns the saturated value (single process, single run, stdout differs)\n\n(a) `python3 -c \"import sys;sys.stdout.buffer.write(b'9'*(1<<24))\" > $P/d4.bin`\n   `( ulimit -v 24000; $C < $P/d4.bin )` vs `( ulimit -v 24000; $R < $P/d4.bin )`\n(b) 16 777 216 × `\\x39`\n(c) **C:** `00000000`, status 0\n(d) **Rust:** `ffffffff`, status 0\n\nRoot cause: glibc's `__vfscanf_internal` buffers the entire digit run in a heap scratch buffer before calling `strtol`; when that allocation fails the conversion is an input failure and `x` keeps its initial `0`. `scan_i32` (`src/driver_impl.rs:146-203`) parses in O(1) memory and always produces the saturated `LONG_MAX`→`int` result. Reproducible threshold map (`ulimit -v` in MiB vs digit-run length):\n\n| digits | 8M | 12M | 16M | 24M | 40M | 64M | 96M |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2^22 | DIFF | same | same | same | same | same | same |\n| 2^24 | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | same | same | same |\n| 2^26 | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF |\n\n(DIFF = C `00000000` / Rust `ffffffff`.) With no `ulimit` at all, both print `ffffffff` up to 64 MiB of digits.\n\n### D5. `EINTR` on the stdin read: C loses the input, Rust retries and reads it\n\n(a) `gcc -O0 -o $P/host3 $P/host3.c -ldl` (installs a `sigaction` SIGALRM handler **without** `SA_RESTART`, then calls the exported `main`)\n   `( sleep 3; printf '7\\n' ) | timeout 10 $P/host3 $P/libc.so n` vs `… $P/librs.so n`\n(b) `\\x37\\x0a`, delivered ~2 s after the alarm interrupts the blocking `read`\n(c) **C:** stdout `00000000\\n` (glibc's `_IO_new_file_underflow` treats the `-1/EINTR` read as a hard error, sets `_IO_ERR_SEEN`, conversion fails, `x` stays 0 — the `7` is never consumed)\n(d) **Rust:** stdout `07000000\\n` (`src/driver_impl.rs:113` retries `ErrorKind::Interrupted`)\n\nWith `SA_RESTART` (`… host3 <lib> r`) both print `07000000` — so the divergence is specifically the EINTR path.\n\n### D6. Exported `driver`/`main` flush to fd 1 immediately instead of leaving the bytes in glibc's `stdout` buffer\n\nRoot cause: `driver_stdout` (`src/driver_impl.rs:221-226`) and `run_main` (`:235-245`) build a `BufWriter` over Rust's own `Stdout` and `flush()` before returning; the C versions only append to glibc's `stdout` buffer, which is flushed by `exit`. Four distinct observable consequences (`$P/host.c`, `$P/host2.c`):\n\n**D6a — output ordering vs a host that uses C stdio.** `printf '1' | $P/host $P/libc.so mixed pm` (host does `printf(\"A\")`, then calls the exported `main`)\n- **C:** `A01000000\\n` · **Rust:** `01000000\\nA`\n\n163 of 288 host-interleave cases diverge this way (specs `pm pmp pdp pdpd pmf m Pm PmP Pdm PdPdP pPmdPp dpd dmpm pmdm`, every stream). Symmetric for `driver`: `pdp` gives C `A44332211\\nA` vs Rust `44332211\\nAA`.\n\n**D6b — host calls `_exit` after the export.** `$P/host2 $P/libc.so dexit` vs `… librs.so dexit`\n- **C:** *(no output — buffer discarded)* · **Rust:** `01000000\\n`. Same for `mexit`.\n\n**D6c — host `fork`s after the export.** `$P/host2 $P/libc.so dfork`\n- **C:** `01000000\\n01000000\\n` (buffer duplicated into the child) · **Rust:** `01000000\\n`. Same for `mfork`.\n\n**D6d — host `abort`s, or reads its own stdout pipe before exiting.** `dabort`: C loses the line, Rust already wrote it. `readpipe`: C reports `pipe-had -1 bytes`, Rust `pipe-had 9 bytes: 01000000`.\n\n### D7. Exported `main` does not share glibc's `stdin` FILE with the host — different *values* printed\n\n**D7a** `printf '1 2 3' | $P/host $P/libc.so mixed gm` (host `getchar()`, then exported `main`)\n- input `\\x31\\x20\\x32\\x20\\x33`; both report `getchar=49`\n- **C:** `02000000\\n` · **Rust:** `00000000\\n`\n\n**D7b** `printf '1' | $P/host $P/libc.so mixed umu` (host `getchar()`+`ungetc()`, then `main`)\n- input `\\x31`\n- **C:** `01000000\\n` · **Rust:** `00000000\\n`\n\n**D7c** `printf '12x34' | $P/host $P/libc.so mixed mgm`\n- **C:** `0c000000\\n22000000\\n` (the host's `getchar` eats the pushed-back `x`) · **Rust:** `0c000000\\n00000000\\n`\n- with `12-34`: **C** `0c000000\\n22000000\\n` · **Rust** `0c000000\\ndeffffff\\n`\n\n**D7d** `feof(stdin)` after `main`: **C** `feof=1` · **Rust** `feof=0` (inputs `\"\"`, `\"1\"`, `\"-\"`).\n\nRoot cause: the pushback/EOF model lives in Rust-private statics + `StdinLock`'s buffer (`src/driver_impl.rs:47-49, 70-91, 234`), invisible to glibc and vice versa.\n\n### D8. Concurrent `driver` calls: C output can tear mid-line, Rust never can (nondeterministic)\n\n`$P/host2 $P/libc.so mthread` — observed 1 of 10 runs producing `0000000000` / `000000` (glibc takes the stdout lock per `printf`, i.e. per 2 hex chars). Rust's `driver`/`run_main` hold the lock for the whole line and emit one 9-byte `write`, so it is always atomic. Reported as a class, not a fixed input.\n\n---\n\n## PROBED AND FOUND IDENTICAL\n\n~84 000 differential cases total; scripts in `$TMPDIR/probe/`.\n\n- **Single-run stdin fuzz of the executables — 39 562 cases, 0 mismatches** (`fuzz_stdin.py` 9 562 + `fuzz_big.py` 30 000). Covered: digit strings length 1–40 (all-9s/all-1s/random/leading-zero/signed), every magnitude around 2^7/2^8/2^15/2^16/2^31/2^32/2^63/2^64/10^19/10^20/10^39/10^40 with 8 junk tails each, `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` ± 1 (glibc's `strtol` saturate-then-truncate is matched exactly, incl. `-9223372036854775808` → `00000000` and `99999999999999999999` → `ffffffff`), all 1/2/3-byte whitespace permutations of ` \\t\\n\\v\\f\\r` in prefix/mid/around-sign positions, `+`/`-`/`--`/`+-`/sign-only, `.5 1e5 0x10 0X10 010 1,000 1_000 inf nan 0b101`, embedded NULs, high bytes `\\x80 \\xff \\xc3\\xa9 \\xe2\\x82\\xac`, CRLF/no-trailing-newline, junk tails, interesting byte at offsets 4094–4098 / 8190–8194 / 65535 / 65536, and 5 multi-MB inputs (2 MiB whitespace, 2 MiB zeros, 2 MiB nines, 1 MiB digits + junk tail, 1 MiB newlines).\n- **`driver` export via dlopen — 44 curated + 4 000 random `i32` values, 0 mismatches**, incl. all `±(1<<k)`, `±((1<<k)-1)` for k=0..31, `INT_MIN/INT_MAX`, `0x7f7f7f7f`, `0x80808080`, `0x01010101`.\n- **ABI abuse of `driver` — 11 calls, identical**: 64-bit garbage in `rdi` (`0x1122334455667788`, `0xFFFFFFFF00000001`, `0xDEADBEEFCAFEBABE`…), extra bogus 2nd arg, called through a varargs pointer.\n- **Exported `main` called 1–5× in one process — ~1 348 cases, 0 mismatches** (48 curated streams × {1,2,3,5} + 1 200 random streams × random N). The `ungetc`/sticky-EOF model in `driver_impl.rs` is faithful for `\"1 2 3\"`, `\"12x34\"`, `\"12-34\"`, `\"-\"`, `\"+x\"`, `\"\"`, `1\\n2\\n3\\n4\\n5\\n`, 4 KiB/8 KiB-boundary streams, over-long numbers, NUL/high-byte streams, and post-EOF repeat calls.\n- **Locale sweep — 253 locales × 55 byte values × 3 positions = 42 504 cases, 0 mismatches**. `LC_ALL`/`LANG` over every installed charset family (utf8, iso8859-1..16, koi8, cp1251, euckr, gb18030, big5, eucjp, sjis, tis620, armscii, georgian, `@`-modifier variants) plus `C`, `POSIX`, `C.utf8`, `invalid.locale`, empty. Bytes `\\x01`–`\\x20`, `\\x7f`, `\\x85`, `\\xa0`, `\\xad`, `\\xb2`, `\\xb9`, `\\xc2`, `\\xe1`, `\\xfe`, `\\xff`, `+ - . , : /`. Rust's hard-coded C-locale `c_isspace` (incl. `\\v`) matches glibc's locale-dependent `ISSPACE`/`ISDIGIT` in every installed locale.\n- **Weird stdin**: `/dev/null`, closed fd 0, a directory, `/dev/zero`, `/dev/full`, a binary file, a write-only fd — all `00000000`, status 0, identical.\n- **Weird stdout**: `/dev/full`, closed fd 1, `/dev/null`, pipe with no reader (both **die on SIGPIPE**; `src/main.rs` correctly restores `SIG_DFL`), `head -c 0`, read-only fd 1, append-mode file, `ulimit -f 0` (both 128+SIGXFSZ=153), closed stderr — identical exit codes and signals.\n- **Slow/partial writers and odd stream types — 11 scenarios, identical**: chunked pipe writes (`\"12\"`+`\"34\"`, `\"1\"+\"2\"+\"3\"`, `\" \"+\"-\"+\"5\"`, `\"12 \"+\"34\"`), writer never closing (both hang → TIMEOUT), `O_NONBLOCK` stdin (both `00000000`), `socketpair` stdin with/without `SHUT_WR`.\n- **pty and FIFO multi-run — identical** (glibc can't seek a tty/pipe, so its read-ahead is lost too): `script -qec '$exe; $exe'` with `1\\n2\\n` → both `01000000 02000000`; with `1 2 3\\n` → both `01000000 00000000`; FIFO twice → both `01000000 00000000`.\n- **Misc — 13 scenarios**: `argc==0` / `argv[0]==\"\"` (both 127), 2 000-element argv, 200 KB env var, `stdbuf -o0/-oL/-i0 -o0`, output to a pty, `RLIMIT_STACK 64k`, `RLIMIT_NOFILE 4`, `RLIMIT_AS 8M`, `RLIMIT_DATA 1M` with 200 KB of digits, stdin==stdout on one `O_RDWR` fd (both `1 2 301000000\\n`, offset 14), all three fds closed.\n- **Return value of the exported `main`**: `0` in both, in every case.\n\n### Ruled out as flaky, not a divergence\n`RLIMIT_CPU = 0` initially showed C `SIGKILL` vs Rust exit-0; 10 repetitions showed the kill landing on the C side twice and the Rust side twice, i.e. pure scheduling noise. `RLIMIT_AS 2M` \"differs\" only because the dynamic-loader error message embeds the (different) executable path.\n\n### Not reachable / not attempted\nA deterministic same-stdout divergence for a *single* run of the executable with an unconstrained environment — I could not construct one; D4 needs an `RLIMIT_AS`, D1–D3 need a co-reader on fd 0, D5–D8 need a host process.\n\n### Note on the existing test suite's blind spot\n`tests/common/mod.rs:397-399` opens a **fresh** `fs::File` per child for file-backed stdin, and each `main`/`driver` call runs in its own forked child, so no test ever shares an fd across two invocations or mixes host glibc stdio with the exports — which is precisely why D1–D3 and D6–D7 pass unnoticed.","usage":{"total_tokens":100095,"tool_uses":37,"duration_ms":1273084},"uuid":"35b946db-7f74-444a-b71e-3b0af4f9b0e1","session_id":"64daaee6-dfd8-4ebc-87be-dd6361eb86bc"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_014gkGHEagq3hVbvtNZNxU7K","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## CONFIRMED DIVERGENCES\n\nAll commands below assume:\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wClUkV/translated_rust\nexport TMPDIR=/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wClUkV/tmp\nP=$TMPDIR/probe          # scratch dir with my probes\nC=$P/c_exe               # gcc -O0 -o $P/c_exe c_src/src/main.c\nR=$PWD/target/debug/driver\n# $P/libc.so  = gcc -shared -fPIC -o $P/libc.so c_src/src/main.c\n# $P/librs.so = rustc --edition 2021 --crate-type cdylib -A warnings -o $P/librs.so src/lib.rs\n```\ngcc 11.5.0, glibc 2.34, rustc 1.94.0, x86-64 Linux.\n\n---\n\n### D1. Shared *seekable* stdin: second invocation reads the wrong data (standalone executable, no dlopen, stdout differs)\n\n(a) `printf '1 2 3' > $P/d1.bin; { $C; $C; } < $P/d1.bin` vs `{ $R; $R; } < $P/d1.bin`\n(b) input `\\x31\\x20\\x32\\x20\\x33` (`\"1 2 3\"`, no newline)\n(c) **C:** `01000000\\n02000000\\n`, status 0\n(d) **Rust:** `01000000\\n00000000\\n`, status 0\n\nRoot cause: glibc's `exit` runs `_IO_cleanup` → `_IO_unbuffer_all` → `_IO_new_file_sync`, which `lseek`s fd 0 back by `_IO_read_ptr - _IO_read_end`. The C program therefore leaves fd 0 positioned exactly one byte past the last character `scanf` consumed (offset 1 here). Rust's `Scanner` reads through `io::stdin().lock()`'s 8 KiB `BufReader` (`src/driver_impl.rs:234`, 1-byte reads at `:102-119`) and never restores the offset, so it swallows the whole file. Systematic fuzz: **526 of 599** shared-seekable-fd cases diverge (`$P/fuzz_seek.py`; full log `$P/seek.out`). Examples of divergent stdout:\n\n| input (hex-escaped) | runs | C stdout | Rust stdout |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `\\x31\\x20\\x32\\x20\\x33` | 3 | `01000000 02000000 03000000` | `01000000 00000000 00000000` |\n| `\\x31\\x0a\\x32\\x0a\\x33\\x0a` | 3 | `01000000 02000000 03000000` | `01000000 00000000 00000000` |\n| `\\x31\\x32\\x2d\\x33\\x34` (`12-34`) | 2 | `0c000000 deffffff` | `0c000000 00000000` |\n| `\\x35\\x20\\x36\\x20\\x37\\x20\\x38\\x20\\x39` | 3 | `05000000 06000000 07000000` | `05000000 00000000 00000000` |\n\nAlso visible with `n=1`: final fd offset is 1 (C) vs 5 (Rust).\n\n### D2. Leftover stdin consumed away from a co-reader on the same fd\n\n(a) `printf '1\\nHELLO\\n' > $P/d2.bin; { $C; cat; } < $P/d2.bin` vs `{ $R; cat; } < $P/d2.bin`\n(b) `\\x31\\x0a\\x48\\x45\\x4c\\x4c\\x4f\\x0a`\n(c) **C:** `01000000\\n\\nHELLO\\n`, status 0\n(d) **Rust:** `01000000\\n`, status 0\n\nSame root cause as D1 (glibc's exit-time seek-back vs Rust's unrestored 8 KiB read-ahead).\n\n### D3. Read-ahead chunk size differs on unseekable stdin: 4096 (glibc) vs 8192 (Rust)\n\n(a) `python3 -c \"import sys;sys.stdout.write('7'+' '*5000+'TAIL')\" > $P/d3.bin`\n   `cat $P/d3.bin | { $C >/dev/null; wc -c; }` vs `cat $P/d3.bin | { $R >/dev/null; wc -c; }`\n(b) `\\x37` + 5000 × `\\x20` + `TAIL` (5005 bytes)\n(c) **C:** `909` (glibc `read`s 4096, leaves the rest in the pipe)\n(d) **Rust:** `0` (Rust `read`s 8192, drains the pipe)\n\nNot seek-related — applies to pipes/FIFOs/sockets too. Requires >4096 bytes queued.\n\n### D4. Huge digit run under a memory limit: C reports an input failure, Rust returns the saturated value (single process, single run, stdout differs)\n\n(a) `python3 -c \"import sys;sys.stdout.buffer.write(b'9'*(1<<24))\" > $P/d4.bin`\n   `( ulimit -v 24000; $C < $P/d4.bin )` vs `( ulimit -v 24000; $R < $P/d4.bin )`\n(b) 16 777 216 × `\\x39`\n(c) **C:** `00000000`, status 0\n(d) **Rust:** `ffffffff`, status 0\n\nRoot cause: glibc's `__vfscanf_internal` buffers the entire digit run in a heap scratch buffer before calling `strtol`; when that allocation fails the conversion is an input failure and `x` keeps its initial `0`. `scan_i32` (`src/driver_impl.rs:146-203`) parses in O(1) memory and always produces the saturated `LONG_MAX`→`int` result. Reproducible threshold map (`ulimit -v` in MiB vs digit-run length):\n\n| digits | 8M | 12M | 16M | 24M | 40M | 64M | 96M |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2^22 | DIFF | same | same | same | same | same | same |\n| 2^24 | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | same | same | same |\n| 2^26 | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF |\n\n(DIFF = C `00000000` / Rust `ffffffff`.) With no `ulimit` at all, both print `ffffffff` up to 64 MiB of digits.\n\n### D5. `EINTR` on the stdin read: C loses the input, Rust retries and reads it\n\n(a) `gcc -O0 -o $P/host3 $P/host3.c -ldl` (installs a `sigaction` SIGALRM handler **without** `SA_RESTART`, then calls the exported `main`)\n   `( sleep 3; printf '7\\n' ) | timeout 10 $P/host3 $P/libc.so n` vs `… $P/librs.so n`\n(b) `\\x37\\x0a`, delivered ~2 s after the alarm interrupts the blocking `read`\n(c) **C:** stdout `00000000\\n` (glibc's `_IO_new_file_underflow` treats the `-1/EINTR` read as a hard error, sets `_IO_ERR_SEEN`, conversion fails, `x` stays 0 — the `7` is never consumed)\n(d) **Rust:** stdout `07000000\\n` (`src/driver_impl.rs:113` retries `ErrorKind::Interrupted`)\n\nWith `SA_RESTART` (`… host3 <lib> r`) both print `07000000` — so the divergence is specifically the EINTR path.\n\n### D6. Exported `driver`/`main` flush to fd 1 immediately instead of leaving the bytes in glibc's `stdout` buffer\n\nRoot cause: `driver_stdout` (`src/driver_impl.rs:221-226`) and `run_main` (`:235-245`) build a `BufWriter` over Rust's own `Stdout` and `flush()` before returning; the C versions only append to glibc's `stdout` buffer, which is flushed by `exit`. Four distinct observable consequences (`$P/host.c`, `$P/host2.c`):\n\n**D6a — output ordering vs a host that uses C stdio.** `printf '1' | $P/host $P/libc.so mixed pm` (host does `printf(\"A\")`, then calls the exported `main`)\n- **C:** `A01000000\\n` · **Rust:** `01000000\\nA`\n\n163 of 288 host-interleave cases diverge this way (specs `pm pmp pdp pdpd pmf m Pm PmP Pdm PdPdP pPmdPp dpd dmpm pmdm`, every stream). Symmetric for `driver`: `pdp` gives C `A44332211\\nA` vs Rust `44332211\\nAA`.\n\n**D6b — host calls `_exit` after the export.** `$P/host2 $P/libc.so dexit` vs `… librs.so dexit`\n- **C:** *(no output — buffer discarded)* · **Rust:** `01000000\\n`. Same for `mexit`.\n\n**D6c — host `fork`s after the export.** `$P/host2 $P/libc.so dfork`\n- **C:** `01000000\\n01000000\\n` (buffer duplicated into the child) · **Rust:** `01000000\\n`. Same for `mfork`.\n\n**D6d — host `abort`s, or reads its own stdout pipe before exiting.** `dabort`: C loses the line, Rust already wrote it. `readpipe`: C reports `pipe-had -1 bytes`, Rust `pipe-had 9 bytes: 01000000`.\n\n### D7. Exported `main` does not share glibc's `stdin` FILE with the host — different *values* printed\n\n**D7a** `printf '1 2 3' | $P/host $P/libc.so mixed gm` (host `getchar()`, then exported `main`)\n- input `\\x31\\x20\\x32\\x20\\x33`; both report `getchar=49`\n- **C:** `02000000\\n` · **Rust:** `00000000\\n`\n\n**D7b** `printf '1' | $P/host $P/libc.so mixed umu` (host `getchar()`+`ungetc()`, then `main`)\n- input `\\x31`\n- **C:** `01000000\\n` · **Rust:** `00000000\\n`\n\n**D7c** `printf '12x34' | $P/host $P/libc.so mixed mgm`\n- **C:** `0c000000\\n22000000\\n` (the host's `getchar` eats the pushed-back `x`) · **Rust:** `0c000000\\n00000000\\n`\n- with `12-34`: **C** `0c000000\\n22000000\\n` · **Rust** `0c000000\\ndeffffff\\n`\n\n**D7d** `feof(stdin)` after `main`: **C** `feof=1` · **Rust** `feof=0` (inputs `\"\"`, `\"1\"`, `\"-\"`).\n\nRoot cause: the pushback/EOF model lives in Rust-private statics + `StdinLock`'s buffer (`src/driver_impl.rs:47-49, 70-91, 234`), invisible to glibc and vice versa.\n\n### D8. Concurrent `driver` calls: C output can tear mid-line, Rust never can (nondeterministic)\n\n`$P/host2 $P/libc.so mthread` — observed 1 of 10 runs producing `0000000000` / `000000` (glibc takes the stdout lock per `printf`, i.e. per 2 hex chars). Rust's `driver`/`run_main` hold the lock for the whole line and emit one 9-byte `write`, so it is always atomic. Reported as a class, not a fixed input.\n\n---\n\n## PROBED AND FOUND IDENTICAL\n\n~84 000 differential cases total; scripts in `$TMPDIR/probe/`.\n\n- **Single-run stdin fuzz of the executables — 39 562 cases, 0 mismatches** (`fuzz_stdin.py` 9 562 + `fuzz_big.py` 30 000). Covered: digit strings length 1–40 (all-9s/all-1s/random/leading-zero/signed), every magnitude around 2^7/2^8/2^15/2^16/2^31/2^32/2^63/2^64/10^19/10^20/10^39/10^40 with 8 junk tails each, `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` ± 1 (glibc's `strtol` saturate-then-truncate is matched exactly, incl. `-9223372036854775808` → `00000000` and `99999999999999999999` → `ffffffff`), all 1/2/3-byte whitespace permutations of ` \\t\\n\\v\\f\\r` in prefix/mid/around-sign positions, `+`/`-`/`--`/`+-`/sign-only, `.5 1e5 0x10 0X10 010 1,000 1_000 inf nan 0b101`, embedded NULs, high bytes `\\x80 \\xff \\xc3\\xa9 \\xe2\\x82\\xac`, CRLF/no-trailing-newline, junk tails, interesting byte at offsets 4094–4098 / 8190–8194 / 65535 / 65536, and 5 multi-MB inputs (2 MiB whitespace, 2 MiB zeros, 2 MiB nines, 1 MiB digits + junk tail, 1 MiB newlines).\n- **`driver` export via dlopen — 44 curated + 4 000 random `i32` values, 0 mismatches**, incl. all `±(1<<k)`, `±((1<<k)-1)` for k=0..31, `INT_MIN/INT_MAX`, `0x7f7f7f7f`, `0x80808080`, `0x01010101`.\n- **ABI abuse of `driver` — 11 calls, identical**: 64-bit garbage in `rdi` (`0x1122334455667788`, `0xFFFFFFFF00000001`, `0xDEADBEEFCAFEBABE`…), extra bogus 2nd arg, called through a varargs pointer.\n- **Exported `main` called 1–5× in one process — ~1 348 cases, 0 mismatches** (48 curated streams × {1,2,3,5} + 1 200 random streams × random N). The `ungetc`/sticky-EOF model in `driver_impl.rs` is faithful for `\"1 2 3\"`, `\"12x34\"`, `\"12-34\"`, `\"-\"`, `\"+x\"`, `\"\"`, `1\\n2\\n3\\n4\\n5\\n`, 4 KiB/8 KiB-boundary streams, over-long numbers, NUL/high-byte streams, and post-EOF repeat calls.\n- **Locale sweep — 253 locales × 55 byte values × 3 positions = 42 504 cases, 0 mismatches**. `LC_ALL`/`LANG` over every installed charset family (utf8, iso8859-1..16, koi8, cp1251, euckr, gb18030, big5, eucjp, sjis, tis620, armscii, georgian, `@`-modifier variants) plus `C`, `POSIX`, `C.utf8`, `invalid.locale`, empty. Bytes `\\x01`–`\\x20`, `\\x7f`, `\\x85`, `\\xa0`, `\\xad`, `\\xb2`, `\\xb9`, `\\xc2`, `\\xe1`, `\\xfe`, `\\xff`, `+ - . , : /`. Rust's hard-coded C-locale `c_isspace` (incl. `\\v`) matches glibc's locale-dependent `ISSPACE`/`ISDIGIT` in every installed locale.\n- **Weird stdin**: `/dev/null`, closed fd 0, a directory, `/dev/zero`, `/dev/full`, a binary file, a write-only fd — all `00000000`, status 0, identical.\n- **Weird stdout**: `/dev/full`, closed fd 1, `/dev/null`, pipe with no reader (both **die on SIGPIPE**; `src/main.rs` correctly restores `SIG_DFL`), `head -c 0`, read-only fd 1, append-mode file, `ulimit -f 0` (both 128+SIGXFSZ=153), closed stderr — identical exit codes and signals.\n- **Slow/partial writers and odd stream types — 11 scenarios, identical**: chunked pipe writes (`\"12\"`+`\"34\"`, `\"1\"+\"2\"+\"3\"`, `\" \"+\"-\"+\"5\"`, `\"12 \"+\"34\"`), writer never closing (both hang → TIMEOUT), `O_NONBLOCK` stdin (both `00000000`), `socketpair` stdin with/without `SHUT_WR`.\n- **pty and FIFO multi-run — identical** (glibc can't seek a tty/pipe, so its read-ahead is lost too): `script -qec '$exe; $exe'` with `1\\n2\\n` → both `01000000 02000000`; with `1 2 3\\n` → both `01000000 00000000`; FIFO twice → both `01000000 00000000`.\n- **Misc — 13 scenarios**: `argc==0` / `argv[0]==\"\"` (both 127), 2 000-element argv, 200 KB env var, `stdbuf -o0/-oL/-i0 -o0`, output to a pty, `RLIMIT_STACK 64k`, `RLIMIT_NOFILE 4`, `RLIMIT_AS 8M`, `RLIMIT_DATA 1M` with 200 KB of digits, stdin==stdout on one `O_RDWR` fd (both `1 2 301000000\\n`, offset 14), all three fds closed.\n- **Return value of the exported `main`**: `0` in both, in every case.\n\n### Ruled out as flaky, not a divergence\n`RLIMIT_CPU = 0` initially showed C `SIGKILL` vs Rust exit-0; 10 repetitions showed the kill landing on the C side twice and the Rust side twice, i.e. pure scheduling noise. `RLIMIT_AS 2M` \"differs\" only because the dynamic-loader error message embeds the (different) executable path.\n\n### Not reachable / not attempted\nA deterministic same-stdout divergence for a *single* run of the executable with an unconstrained environment — I could not construct one; D4 needs an `RLIMIT_AS`, D1–D3 need a co-reader on fd 0, D5–D8 need a host process.\n\n### Note on the existing test suite's blind spot\n`tests/common/mod.rs:397-399` opens a **fresh** `fs::File` per child for file-backed stdin, and each `main`/`driver` call runs in its own forked child, so no test ever shares an fd across two invocations or mixes host glibc stdio with the exports — which is precisely why D1–D3 and D6–D7 pass unnoticed."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aceb7482d82b1d449 (use SendMessage with to: 'aceb7482d82b1d449', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 105636\ntool_uses: 37\nduration_ms: 1273088</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64daaee6-dfd8-4ebc-87be-dd6361eb86bc","uuid":"73593e3e-e014-40b1-97f5-d6a82418634f","timestamp":"2026-08-18T10:08:54.414Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are an adversarial divergence hunter for a C-to-Rust translation. Your ONLY job is to FIND INPUTS/CONDITIONS WHERE THE RUST BEHAVES DIFFERENTLY FROM THE C. Report them; do not fix anything.\n\nWorking dir: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wClUkV/translated_rust\n\n- C ground truth: c_src/src/main.c (41 lines: static print_hex, void driver(int), int main(void) that does `int x=0; scanf(\"%d\",&x); driver(x);`)\n- Rust translation: src/driver_impl.rs (all logic), src/lib.rs (#[no_mangle] `driver` and `main` exports), src/main.rs (binary).\n\nHARD RULES:\n- Do NOT modify ANY file under the working dir (no edits to c_src/, src/, tests/, Cargo.toml, *.md). Read-only on the repo.\n- Write scratch files ONLY under \"$TMPDIR\" (already set; use it, never /tmp).\n- Use timeouts on every command (e.g. `timeout 300 ...`). Never run a command longer than 600s.\n- Do not run `cargo test` on the existing suite (it is already known to pass); write your OWN independent probes.\n\nAlready-built artifacts you can use (rebuild into $TMPDIR if you prefer):\n- C executable: c_src/build/driver   (built with cmake; rebuild: `gcc -o $TMPDIR/c_exe c_src/src/main.c`)\n- Rust executable: `timeout 300 cargo build --offline` then target/debug/driver\n- C shared lib: `gcc -shared -fPIC -o $TMPDIR/libc.so c_src/src/main.c`  (exports `driver`, `main`)\n- Rust shared lib: `timeout 300 rustc --edition 2021 --crate-type cdylib -A warnings -o $TMPDIR/librs.so src/lib.rs` (exports `driver`, `main`)\n\nYOUR TASK — try hard to break it. Ideas (do these AND invent your own):\n1. Fuzz the executables: feed thousands of random byte strings on stdin to both binaries and diff stdout+exit status. Cover: random ASCII, random bytes, digit strings of every length 1..40, signs, whitespace mixes (space/tab/nl/vt/ff/cr), leading zeros, huge numbers (magnitudes around 2^31, 2^32, 2^63, 2^64, 10^40), junk tails, inputs with NULs, multi-MB inputs, inputs whose interesting byte lands exactly at offsets 4095/4096/4097/8191/8192/8193, no trailing newline, CRLF, etc. Write a shell or python3 loop; report ANY mismatch with the exact input bytes (hex-escaped) and both outputs.\n2. Fuzz the `driver` export through dlopen in both .so files (write your own small C program in $TMPDIR that dlopens each .so and calls driver with values you choose, or use python3 ctypes) for many int values incl. all boundary/bit-pattern shapes; diff the printed bytes.\n3. Exercise the exported `main` symbol MULTIPLE times in one process (dlopen + call main 2..5 times) with streams like \"1 2 3\", \"12x34\", \"12-34\", \"1\", \"\", \"junk\", and diff. (This is known to be modelled via glibc ungetc/EOF state — try to find shapes where it still differs.)\n4. Weird fds: stdin closed, stdin = directory, stdin = /dev/null, stdin = /dev/zero (careful: infinite; use `timeout 5`), stdout closed, stdout = /dev/full, stdout = pipe with no reader (SIGPIPE), stdout = append-mode file. Compare exit codes and signals.\n5. Environment: LC_ALL/LANG/LC_NUMERIC exotic values; huge argv/env; running under `stdbuf`; stdout to a pty if you can (`script -qec ... /dev/null`).\n6. Anything else that could differ: buffering, partial writes, timing of flush relative to output, return value/exit status, signal disposition, resource limits (`ulimit -f 0`? do not break the machine), etc.\n\nReport format: a numbered list of CONFIRMED divergences, each with (a) exact reproduction command, (b) the input bytes hex-escaped, (c) C output/status, (d) Rust output/status. Then a list of things you probed and found IDENTICAL (brief). If you find no divergence, say so explicitly and list the probe coverage with the number of cases per probe. Be precise and honest; do not speculate about divergences you did not actually reproduce.","agentId":"aceb7482d82b1d449","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## CONFIRMED DIVERGENCES\n\nAll commands below assume:\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wClUkV/translated_rust\nexport TMPDIR=/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wClUkV/tmp\nP=$TMPDIR/probe          # scratch dir with my probes\nC=$P/c_exe               # gcc -O0 -o $P/c_exe c_src/src/main.c\nR=$PWD/target/debug/driver\n# $P/libc.so  = gcc -shared -fPIC -o $P/libc.so c_src/src/main.c\n# $P/librs.so = rustc --edition 2021 --crate-type cdylib -A warnings -o $P/librs.so src/lib.rs\n```\ngcc 11.5.0, glibc 2.34, rustc 1.94.0, x86-64 Linux.\n\n---\n\n### D1. Shared *seekable* stdin: second invocation reads the wrong data (standalone executable, no dlopen, stdout differs)\n\n(a) `printf '1 2 3' > $P/d1.bin; { $C; $C; } < $P/d1.bin` vs `{ $R; $R; } < $P/d1.bin`\n(b) input `\\x31\\x20\\x32\\x20\\x33` (`\"1 2 3\"`, no newline)\n(c) **C:** `01000000\\n02000000\\n`, status 0\n(d) **Rust:** `01000000\\n00000000\\n`, status 0\n\nRoot cause: glibc's `exit` runs `_IO_cleanup` → `_IO_unbuffer_all` → `_IO_new_file_sync`, which `lseek`s fd 0 back by `_IO_read_ptr - _IO_read_end`. The C program therefore leaves fd 0 positioned exactly one byte past the last character `scanf` consumed (offset 1 here). Rust's `Scanner` reads through `io::stdin().lock()`'s 8 KiB `BufReader` (`src/driver_impl.rs:234`, 1-byte reads at `:102-119`) and never restores the offset, so it swallows the whole file. Systematic fuzz: **526 of 599** shared-seekable-fd cases diverge (`$P/fuzz_seek.py`; full log `$P/seek.out`). Examples of divergent stdout:\n\n| input (hex-escaped) | runs | C stdout | Rust stdout |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `\\x31\\x20\\x32\\x20\\x33` | 3 | `01000000 02000000 03000000` | `01000000 00000000 00000000` |\n| `\\x31\\x0a\\x32\\x0a\\x33\\x0a` | 3 | `01000000 02000000 03000000` | `01000000 00000000 00000000` |\n| `\\x31\\x32\\x2d\\x33\\x34` (`12-34`) | 2 | `0c000000 deffffff` | `0c000000 00000000` |\n| `\\x35\\x20\\x36\\x20\\x37\\x20\\x38\\x20\\x39` | 3 | `05000000 06000000 07000000` | `05000000 00000000 00000000` |\n\nAlso visible with `n=1`: final fd offset is 1 (C) vs 5 (Rust).\n\n### D2. Leftover stdin consumed away from a co-reader on the same fd\n\n(a) `printf '1\\nHELLO\\n' > $P/d2.bin; { $C; cat; } < $P/d2.bin` vs `{ $R; cat; } < $P/d2.bin`\n(b) `\\x31\\x0a\\x48\\x45\\x4c\\x4c\\x4f\\x0a`\n(c) **C:** `01000000\\n\\nHELLO\\n`, status 0\n(d) **Rust:** `01000000\\n`, status 0\n\nSame root cause as D1 (glibc's exit-time seek-back vs Rust's unrestored 8 KiB read-ahead).\n\n### D3. Read-ahead chunk size differs on unseekable stdin: 4096 (glibc) vs 8192 (Rust)\n\n(a) `python3 -c \"import sys;sys.stdout.write('7'+' '*5000+'TAIL')\" > $P/d3.bin`\n   `cat $P/d3.bin | { $C >/dev/null; wc -c; }` vs `cat $P/d3.bin | { $R >/dev/null; wc -c; }`\n(b) `\\x37` + 5000 × `\\x20` + `TAIL` (5005 bytes)\n(c) **C:** `909` (glibc `read`s 4096, leaves the rest in the pipe)\n(d) **Rust:** `0` (Rust `read`s 8192, drains the pipe)\n\nNot seek-related — applies to pipes/FIFOs/sockets too. Requires >4096 bytes queued.\n\n### D4. Huge digit run under a memory limit: C reports an input failure, Rust returns the saturated value (single process, single run, stdout differs)\n\n(a) `python3 -c \"import sys;sys.stdout.buffer.write(b'9'*(1<<24))\" > $P/d4.bin`\n   `( ulimit -v 24000; $C < $P/d4.bin )` vs `( ulimit -v 24000; $R < $P/d4.bin )`\n(b) 16 777 216 × `\\x39`\n(c) **C:** `00000000`, status 0\n(d) **Rust:** `ffffffff`, status 0\n\nRoot cause: glibc's `__vfscanf_internal` buffers the entire digit run in a heap scratch buffer before calling `strtol`; when that allocation fails the conversion is an input failure and `x` keeps its initial `0`. `scan_i32` (`src/driver_impl.rs:146-203`) parses in O(1) memory and always produces the saturated `LONG_MAX`→`int` result. Reproducible threshold map (`ulimit -v` in MiB vs digit-run length):\n\n| digits | 8M | 12M | 16M | 24M | 40M | 64M | 96M |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2^22 | DIFF | same | same | same | same | same | same |\n| 2^24 | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | same | same | same |\n| 2^26 | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF | DIFF |\n\n(DIFF = C `00000000` / Rust `ffffffff`.) With no `ulimit` at all, both print `ffffffff` up to 64 MiB of digits.\n\n### D5. `EINTR` on the stdin read: C loses the input, Rust retries and reads it\n\n(a) `gcc -O0 -o $P/host3 $P/host3.c -ldl` (installs a `sigaction` SIGALRM handler **without** `SA_RESTART`, then calls the exported `main`)\n   `( sleep 3; printf '7\\n' ) | timeout 10 $P/host3 $P/libc.so n` vs `… $P/librs.so n`\n(b) `\\x37\\x0a`, delivered ~2 s after the alarm interrupts the blocking `read`\n(c) **C:** stdout `00000000\\n` (glibc's `_IO_new_file_underflow` treats the `-1/EINTR` read as a hard error, sets `_IO_ERR_SEEN`, conversion fails, `x` stays 0 — the `7` is never consumed)\n(d) **Rust:** stdout `07000000\\n` (`src/driver_impl.rs:113` retries `ErrorKind::Interrupted`)\n\nWith `SA_RESTART` (`… host3 <lib> r`) both print `07000000` — so the divergence is specifically the EINTR path.\n\n### D6. Exported `driver`/`main` flush to fd 1 immediately instead of leaving the bytes in glibc's `stdout` buffer\n\nRoot cause: `driver_stdout` (`src/driver_impl.rs:221-226`) and `run_main` (`:235-245`) build a `BufWriter` over Rust's own `Stdout` and `flush()` before returning; the C versions only append to glibc's `stdout` buffer, which is flushed by `exit`. Four distinct observable consequences (`$P/host.c`, `$P/host2.c`):\n\n**D6a — output ordering vs a host that uses C stdio.** `printf '1' | $P/host $P/libc.so mixed pm` (host does `printf(\"A\")`, then calls the exported `main`)\n- **C:** `A01000000\\n` · **Rust:** `01000000\\nA`\n\n163 of 288 host-interleave cases diverge this way (specs `pm pmp pdp pdpd pmf m Pm PmP Pdm PdPdP pPmdPp dpd dmpm pmdm`, every stream). Symmetric for `driver`: `pdp` gives C `A44332211\\nA` vs Rust `44332211\\nAA`.\n\n**D6b — host calls `_exit` after the export.** `$P/host2 $P/libc.so dexit` vs `… librs.so dexit`\n- **C:** *(no output — buffer discarded)* · **Rust:** `01000000\\n`. Same for `mexit`.\n\n**D6c — host `fork`s after the export.** `$P/host2 $P/libc.so dfork`\n- **C:** `01000000\\n01000000\\n` (buffer duplicated into the child) · **Rust:** `01000000\\n`. Same for `mfork`.\n\n**D6d — host `abort`s, or reads its own stdout pipe before exiting.** `dabort`: C loses the line, Rust already wrote it. `readpipe`: C reports `pipe-had -1 bytes`, Rust `pipe-had 9 bytes: 01000000`.\n\n### D7. Exported `main` does not share glibc's `stdin` FILE with the host — different *values* printed\n\n**D7a** `printf '1 2 3' | $P/host $P/libc.so mixed gm` (host `getchar()`, then exported `main`)\n- input `\\x31\\x20\\x32\\x20\\x33`; both report `getchar=49`\n- **C:** `02000000\\n` · **Rust:** `00000000\\n`\n\n**D7b** `printf '1' | $P/host $P/libc.so mixed umu` (host `getchar()`+`ungetc()`, then `main`)\n- input `\\x31`\n- **C:** `01000000\\n` · **Rust:** `00000000\\n`\n\n**D7c** `printf '12x34' | $P/host $P/libc.so mixed mgm`\n- **C:** `0c000000\\n22000000\\n` (the host's `getchar` eats the pushed-back `x`) · **Rust:** `0c000000\\n00000000\\n`\n- with `12-34`: **C** `0c000000\\n22000000\\n` · **Rust** `0c000000\\ndeffffff\\n`\n\n**D7d** `feof(stdin)` after `main`: **C** `feof=1` · **Rust** `feof=0` (inputs `\"\"`, `\"1\"`, `\"-\"`).\n\nRoot cause: the pushback/EOF model lives in Rust-private statics + `StdinLock`'s buffer (`src/driver_impl.rs:47-49, 70-91, 234`), invisible to glibc and vice versa.\n\n### D8. Concurrent `driver` calls: C output can tear mid-line, Rust never can (nondeterministic)\n\n`$P/host2 $P/libc.so mthread` — observed 1 of 10 runs producing `0000000000` / `000000` (glibc takes the stdout lock per `printf`, i.e. per 2 hex chars). Rust's `driver`/`run_main` hold the lock for the whole line and emit one 9-byte `write`, so it is always atomic. Reported as a class, not a fixed input.\n\n---\n\n## PROBED AND FOUND IDENTICAL\n\n~84 000 differential cases total; scripts in `$TMPDIR/probe/`.\n\n- **Single-run stdin fuzz of the executables — 39 562 cases, 0 mismatches** (`fuzz_stdin.py` 9 562 + `fuzz_big.py` 30 000). Covered: digit strings length 1–40 (all-9s/all-1s/random/leading-zero/signed), every magnitude around 2^7/2^8/2^15/2^16/2^31/2^32/2^63/2^64/10^19/10^20/10^39/10^40 with 8 junk tails each, `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` ± 1 (glibc's `strtol` saturate-then-truncate is matched exactly, incl. `-9223372036854775808` → `00000000` and `99999999999999999999` → `ffffffff`), all 1/2/3-byte whitespace permutations of ` \\t\\n\\v\\f\\r` in prefix/mid/around-sign positions, `+`/`-`/`--`/`+-`/sign-only, `.5 1e5 0x10 0X10 010 1,000 1_000 inf nan 0b101`, embedded NULs, high bytes `\\x80 \\xff \\xc3\\xa9 \\xe2\\x82\\xac`, CRLF/no-trailing-newline, junk tails, interesting byte at offsets 4094–4098 / 8190–8194 / 65535 / 65536, and 5 multi-MB inputs (2 MiB whitespace, 2 MiB zeros, 2 MiB nines, 1 MiB digits + junk tail, 1 MiB newlines).\n- **`driver` export via dlopen — 44 curated + 4 000 random `i32` values, 0 mismatches**, incl. all `±(1<<k)`, `±((1<<k)-1)` for k=0..31, `INT_MIN/INT_MAX`, `0x7f7f7f7f`, `0x80808080`, `0x01010101`.\n- **ABI abuse of `driver` — 11 calls, identical**: 64-bit garbage in `rdi` (`0x1122334455667788`, `0xFFFFFFFF00000001`, `0xDEADBEEFCAFEBABE`…), extra bogus 2nd arg, called through a varargs pointer.\n- **Exported `main` called 1–5× in one process — ~1 348 cases, 0 mismatches** (48 curated streams × {1,2,3,5} + 1 200 random streams × random N). The `ungetc`/sticky-EOF model in `driver_impl.rs` is faithful for `\"1 2 3\"`, `\"12x34\"`, `\"12-34\"`, `\"-\"`, `\"+x\"`, `\"\"`, `1\\n2\\n3\\n4\\n5\\n`, 4 KiB/8 KiB-boundary streams, over-long numbers, NUL/high-byte streams, and post-EOF repeat calls.\n- **Locale sweep — 253 locales × 55 byte values × 3 positions = 42 504 cases, 0 mismatches**. `LC_ALL`/`LANG` over every installed charset family (utf8, iso8859-1..16, koi8, cp1251, euckr, gb18030, big5, eucjp, sjis, tis620, armscii, georgian, `@`-modifier variants) plus `C`, `POSIX`, `C.utf8`, `invalid.locale`, empty. Bytes `\\x01`–`\\x20`, `\\x7f`, `\\x85`, `\\xa0`, `\\xad`, `\\xb2`, `\\xb9`, `\\xc2`, `\\xe1`, `\\xfe`, `\\xff`, `+ - . , : /`. Rust's hard-coded C-locale `c_isspace` (incl. `\\v`) matches glibc's locale-dependent `ISSPACE`/`ISDIGIT` in every installed locale.\n- **Weird stdin**: `/dev/null`, closed fd 0, a directory, `/dev/zero`, `/dev/full`, a binary file, a write-only fd — all `00000000`, status 0, identical.\n- **Weird stdout**: `/dev/full`, closed fd 1, `/dev/null`, pipe with no reader (both **die on SIGPIPE**; `src/main.rs` correctly restores `SIG_DFL`), `head -c 0`, read-only fd 1, append-mode file, `ulimit -f 0` (both 128+SIGXFSZ=153), closed stderr — identical exit codes and signals.\n- **Slow/partial writers and odd stream types — 11 scenarios, identical**: chunked pipe writes (`\"12\"`+`\"34\"`, `\"1\"+\"2\"+\"3\"`, `\" \"+\"-\"+\"5\"`, `\"12 \"+\"34\"`), writer never closing (both hang → TIMEOUT), `O_NONBLOCK` stdin (both `00000000`), `socketpair` stdin with/without `SHUT_WR`.\n- **pty and FIFO multi-run — identical** (glibc can't seek a tty/pipe, so its read-ahead is lost too): `script -qec '$exe; $exe'` with `1\\n2\\n` → both `01000000 02000000`; with `1 2 3\\n` → both `01000000 00000000`; FIFO twice → both `01000000 00000000`.\n- **Misc — 13 scenarios**: `argc==0` / `argv[0]==\"\"` (both 127), 2 000-element argv, 200 KB env var, `stdbuf -o0/-oL/-i0 -o0`, output to a pty, `RLIMIT_STACK 64k`, `RLIMIT_NOFILE 4`, `RLIMIT_AS 8M`, `RLIMIT_DATA 1M` with 200 KB of digits, stdin==stdout on one `O_RDWR` fd (both `1 2 301000000\\n`, offset 14), all three fds closed.\n- **Return value of the exported `main`**: `0` in both, in every case.\n\n### Ruled out as flaky, not a divergence\n`RLIMIT_CPU = 0` initially showed C `SIGKILL` vs Rust exit-0; 10 repetitions showed the kill landing on the C side twice and the Rust side twice, i.e. pure scheduling noise. `RLIMIT_AS 2M` \"differs\" only because the dynamic-loader error message embeds the (different) executable path.\n\n### Not reachable / not attempted\nA deterministic same-stdout divergence for a *single* run of the executable with an unconstrained environment — I could not construct one; D4 needs an `RLIMIT_AS`, D1–D3 need a co-reader on fd 0, D5–D8 need a host process.\n\n### Note on the existing test suite's blind spot\n`tests/common/mod.rs:397-399` opens a **fresh** `fs::File` per child for file-backed stdin, and each `main`/`driver` call runs in its own forked child, so no test ever shares an fd across two invocations or mixes host glibc stdio with the exports — which is precisely why D1–D3 and D6–D7 pass unnoticed."}],"resolvedModel":"global.anthropic.claude-