{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01UaafVEJbpooji3b7x1metQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# ERRORS.md — error-surface table (Phase A → Phase C)\n2\t\n3\t## Mechanical derivation\n4\t\n5\t`c_src/src/main.c` is 41 lines and contains **no** classic error plumbing.\n6\tGrepped for every rejection idiom:\n7\t\n8\t```\n9\t$ grep -nE 'RETURN_ERROR|return *-1|return *NULL|assert|errno|exit\\(|abort|ERROR|goto' c_src/src/main.c\n10\t(no matches)\n11\t$ grep -nE 'return|if|while|for|<|>|==|!=' c_src/src/main.c | grep -v '^[0-9]*://'\n12\t24:#include <stdio.h>\n13\t27:    for (int i = 0; i < len; i++) {     # the only comparison in the file\n14\t41:    return 0;                          # the only return  (main, unconditional)\n15\t```\n16\t\n17\tSo there is exactly **one** conditional (`i < len`), **one** `return` (an\n18\tunconditional `return 0`), no `assert`, no null check, no range check and no\n19\tmin/max constant. All rejection behaviour therefore lives in\n20\t\n21\t1. the library call `scanf(\"%d\", &x)` (whose result is **never checked**),\n22\t2. the ignored return values of `printf`/`putchar`,\n23\t3. the implicit `long`→`int` narrowing glibc performs when storing `%d`,\n24\t4. the *absence* of validation in `print_hex` / `driver` (every bit pattern is\n25\t   accepted), and\n26\t5. the process-level signal disposition inherited by the C program.\n27\t\n28\tEach distinct rejection/failure mode below is one row. \"expected C result\" is\n29\tthe measured behaviour of the C build (`c_src/build/driver` and\n30\t`build_c/libdriver_c.so`) on this platform (x86-64 Linux, glibc 2.34+,\n31\t`sizeof(int)==4`, `sizeof(long)==8`, little endian).\n32\t\n33\t## Table\n34\t\n35\t| # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | test | [x] |\n36\t|---|----------|---------------------------------------------|-------------------|------|-----|\n37\t| E1 | `main` / `scanf(\"%d\")` | input failure: stdin is empty (immediate EOF) | `scanf` returns `EOF`; `x` keeps its initialiser `0`; stdout `00000000\\n`; exit 0 | `e1_empty_stdin_input_failure` | [x] |\n38\t| E2 | `main` / `scanf(\"%d\")` | input failure: stdin holds only whitespace (` `, `\\t`, `\\n`, `\\v`, `\\f`, `\\r`, and mixes) then EOF | `EOF`; `x==0`; `00000000\\n`; exit 0 | `e2_whitespace_only_input_failure` | [x] |\n39\t| E3 | `main` / `scanf(\"%d\")` | input failure: read error on stdin — fd 0 closed (`EBADF`), and fd 0 open on a directory (`EISDIR`) | `EOF`; `x==0`; `00000000\\n`; exit 0 | `e3_unreadable_stdin_input_failure` | [x] |\n40\t| E4 | `main` / `scanf(\"%d\")` | matching failure: first non-whitespace byte cannot start a `%d` conversion (`a`, `.`, `,`, `/`, `:`, `\\`, `x`, `#`, `\\0`, `\\x80`, `\\xff`, `-`+letter…) | returns `0`; `x` untouched (`0`); `00000000\\n`; exit 0; the offending byte is handed back to the stream, which *is* observable — a later conversion re-reads it (CONFIGS.md C28) and it counts towards the exit-time seek-back (C30) | `e4_leading_non_numeric_matching_failure` | [x] |\n41\t| E5 | `main` / `scanf(\"%d\")` | matching failure: sign then EOF (`\"-\"`, `\"+\"`, `\"   -\"`) | `0`; `x==0`; `00000000\\n`; exit 0 | `e5_sign_then_eof_matching_failure` | [x] |\n42\t| E6 | `main` / `scanf(\"%d\")` | matching failure: sign then a non-digit (`\"-a\"`, `\"+ 5\"`, `\"- 5\"`, `\"--5\"`, `\"+-5\"`, `\"-.\"`, `\"-\\n5\"`) | `0`; `x==0`; `00000000\\n`; exit 0 | `e6_sign_then_non_digit_matching_failure` | [x] |\n43\t| E7 | `main` / `scanf(\"%d\")` | out of range, positive: digit string with magnitude `> LONG_MAX` (`9223372036854775808`, `2^64`, 10⁴⁰, 5000 nines) | glibc's internal `strtol` saturates at `LONG_MAX` (`ERANGE`, ignored); stored as `(int)LONG_MAX == -1`; stdout `ffffffff\\n`; exit 0 | `e7_positive_overflow_saturates_to_long_max` | [x] |\n44\t| E8 | `main` / `scanf(\"%d\")` | out of range, negative: `-` and magnitude `> LONG_MAX` (`-9223372036854775809`, `-2^64`, `-`10⁴⁰) | saturates at `LONG_MIN`; stored as `(int)LONG_MIN == 0`; stdout `00000000\\n`; exit 0 | `e8_negative_overflow_saturates_to_long_min` | [x] |\n45\t| E9 | `main` / `scanf(\"%d\")` | one step past the documented `int` range but inside `long`: `2147483648`, `-2147483649`, `4294967295`, `4294967296`, `9223372036854775807`, `-9223372036854775808` | no error at all — the `long` result is silently narrowed to the low 32 bits (`00000080`, `ffffff7f`, `ffffffff`, `00000000`, `ffffffff`, `00000000`) | `e9_int_range_overflow_is_silently_narrowed` | [x] |\n46\t| E10 | `main` | `scanf`'s return value (`EOF`/`0`/`1`) is never inspected | no failure is ever reported: exactly 9 bytes of output and exit status 0 for **every** input, valid or not | `e10_scanf_result_never_checked` | [x] |\n47\t| E11 | `driver`, `main` | stdout write error: fd 1 is closed (`EBADF`) | `printf`/`putchar` failures ignored; function returns normally; `main` returns 0; no output; no crash | `e11_stdout_closed_is_ignored` | [x] |\n48\t| E12 | `driver`, `main` | stdout write error: fd 1 is `/dev/full` (`ENOSPC` at flush) | error ignored; returns normally / exit 0 | `e12_stdout_enospc_is_ignored` | [x] |\n49\t| E13 | `driver`, `main` | stdout is a pipe whose read end is closed, with the default `SIGPIPE` disposition (as in any C process) | process is killed by `SIGPIPE` (signal 13); no output | `e13_stdout_epipe_raises_sigpipe` (exports), `e13b_executables_die_from_sigpipe_too` (the real programs — Rust's runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN`, so `src/main.rs` restores `SIG_DFL`) | [x] |\n50\t| E14 | `print_hex` | `len <= 0` (`0`, negative) — no length validation exists | loop body never runs, only `\"\\n\"` is printed | unreachable: `driver` hard-codes `sizeof(int)==4` and `print_hex` is `static` (absent from `nm -D`), so no FFI caller can trigger it; documented as not-exported in `SYMBOLS.md` | [x] |\n51\t| E15 | `print_hex` | `p == NULL` with `len > 0` — no null check exists | undefined behaviour in C (SIGSEGV in