{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_013cAgWJboEvc4YgaiWMPEPz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# CONFIGS.md — configuration-surface table (Phase A → Phase B)\n2\t\n3\t## Mechanical derivation of the axes\n4\t\n5\tEverything below is derived from `c_src/src/main.c` and `c_src/CMakeLists.txt`.\n6\t\n7\t* **Build-time configuration.** `CMakeLists.txt` is\n8\t  `cmake_minimum_required(3.10)` + `project(driver)` + `add_executable(driver\n9\t  src/main.c)` — no `option()`, no `target_compile_definitions`, no\n10\t  `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` branches. The C source contains **zero** `#ifdef`/`#if`\n11\t  (`grep -c '#if\\|#ifdef\\|#ifndef' c_src/src/main.c` → 0). `Cargo.toml` has\n12\t  **no `[features]` section**, so the only feature combination is the empty one\n13\t  (verified with `cargo check --no-default-features` and plain `cargo check`).\n14\t  The two remaining build configurations that *can* change behaviour on the\n15\t  Rust side are the cargo profiles, because `[profile.release]` sets\n16\t  `panic = \"abort\"` and enables optimisation; both are covered\n17\t  (`opt-level=0`/`debug-assertions=on` and `opt-level=3`/`panic=abort`).\n18\t* **Runtime options/flags.** None. `main` takes no `argc`/`argv`, reads no\n19\t  environment variable, has no locale call, no getopt, no config file. The only\n20\t  runtime inputs are (a) the `int` argument of `driver` and (b) the byte stream\n21\t  on stdin consumed by `scanf(\"%d\", &x)`.\n22\t* **Public entry points** (`nm -D` on the C `.so`, see `SYMBOLS.md`):\n23\t  `driver` (the low-level entry point — call it directly, do not only drive it\n24\t  through `main`) and `main` (the one-shot wrapper: `scanf` + `driver`).\n25\t  `print_hex` is `static`, exercised through `driver`.\n26\t* **Input shapes the code actually distinguishes.**\n27\t  * `driver`/`print_hex`: the 4 bytes of the `int` in host order; `%02x`\n28\t    formatting distinguishes bytes `< 0x10` (zero-padded nibble) from\n29\t    `>= 0x10`, and `0x00`/`0xff` are the loop's extremes. Byte order matters\n30\t    (`&x` reinterpreted as `unsigned char*`).\n31\t  * `main`: presence/kind/length of leading whitespace, sign (`none`/`+`/`-`),\n32\t    digit count (1 … thousands), leading zeros, value magnitude relative to the\n33\t    `int`/`unsigned int`/`long` boundaries, trailing junk, trailing newline or\n34\t    not, stdin being a regular file vs a pipe, and input longer than the stream\n35\t    buffer (glibc sizes it from `st_blksize` of fd 0, i.e. 4 KiB here, capped by\n36\t    `BUFSIZ` = 8 KiB; the translation derives it the same way, so both consume\n37\t    the same number of bytes per `read`).\n38\t* **Stream state.** `driver` is stateless apart from the stdout buffer, but the\n39\t  conversion in `main` is not — and the state lives in the *stream*, not in the\n40\t  call:\n41\t  * `scanf` hands the byte it stopped on back to `stdin` (`ungetc`), so calling\n42\t    the exported `main` again continues exactly there (row C28: `\"12x34\"` ⇒ the\n43\t    second conversion sees `x`, `\"12-34\"` ⇒ it sees `-34`);\n44\t  * `scanf` reads *ahead* into the stream buffer, and at process exit glibc's\n45\t    `_IO_cleanup` seeks a seekable fd 0 back over the unconsumed part, so the\n46\t    next reader of the descriptor sees the same bytes (row C30);\n47\t  * the granularity of the stdout lock (one lock per `printf`) decides how\n48\t    concurrent callers may interleave (row C31).\n49\t  The translation therefore models the stream itself (`C_STDIN`, the `atexit`\n50\t  seek-back and `CStdout` in `src/driver_impl.rs`) instead of using\n51\t  `io::stdin()`/`io::stdout()`'s private buffering.\n52\t* **Process environment.** The C code never calls `setlocale`, so `LC_*`/`LANG`\n53\t  must not change anything (row C29); the `SIGPIPE` disposition inherited from\n54\t  the process start-up is part of this axis too (see ERRORS.md E13).\n55\t\n56\tThe rows below are the pruned cross-product of those axes: