checked_add`, `-(unread as i64)` bounded by 8193 — all safe under `overflow-checks=on`.\n* **SYMBOLS.md** exact: C `.so` defines `driver`,`main`; Rust `.so` the same and nothing else; Rust undefined set is exactly `_ITM_*`/`_Unwind_*`/`__cxa_*`/`__tls_get_addr`/`pthread_key_*`/libc; `DT_NEEDED` = `libgcc_s.so.1, libc.so.6, ld-linux-x86-64.so.2` as written.\n* **Item 6 totals** (`cargo test --offline`, exit 0): `src/main.rs` 0, `driver_valid` 10, `error_paths` 20, `executables` 4, `main_valid` 15, `repeat_main` 7, `smoke` 4, `stdin_stream` 11, `symbols` 4 = **75 passed, 0 failed**. `Cargo.toml` has **no `[features]`** and `libloading = \"0.8\"` under `[dev-dependencies]` (line 22-23). `cargo check --offline --no-default-features` and plain `cargo check --offline` both succeed, as CONFIGS.md:13 claims.\n* **Item 7**: `grep -rn \"imp::|driver_impl|use driver\" tests/` → no matches; every comparison goes through `libloading`-resolved `driver`/`main` or the cargo-built executable. Absolute (non-differential) anchors do exist so a both-sides regression cannot pass silently: `known_good_outputs`, `c24_*` (`01000000\\n07000000\\nffffffff\\n`), `c33_stdout_is_a_terminal` (`\\r\\n` translation), `c30_helpers_observe_the_documented_c_behaviour` (offset `1`, `len-4096` bytes left in the pipe), and every `expect_output` in `error_paths.rs`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a93b9e9a7fd43e2af (use SendMessage with to: 'a93b9e9a7fd43e2af', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 212786\ntool_uses: 62\nduration_ms: 1321764</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64daaee6-dfd8-4ebc-87be-dd6361eb86bc","uuid":"0fca8e0f-beba-47ea-839f-3c7f2d584ef7","timestamp":"2026-08-18T10:58:47.628Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are re-auditing the verification artifacts of a C-to-Rust translation after a round of fixes. Check FACTUAL ACCURACY and COMPLETENESS. Report problems; fix nothing.\n\nWorking dir: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wClUkV/translated_rust\n\nArtifacts: SYMBOLS.md, ERRORS.md (rows E1–E18), CONFIGS.md (rows C1–C33 + a \"Deliberately not emulated\" section), tests/*.rs (9 test binaries, 75 #[test] fns), src/driver_impl.rs, src/lib.rs, src/main.rs, c_src/src/main.c, Cargo.toml, scripts/verify_all.sh.\n\nHARD RULES\n- Do NOT modify ANY file in the working dir (read-only). Scratch files ONLY under \"$TMPDIR\" (never /tmp).\n- `timeout` on every command; nothing over 600s.\n- Do not run scripts/verify_all.sh (it writes inside the working dir). You MAY run `timeout 600 cargo test --offline`.\n\nCHECKLIST — verify mechanically, report every inaccuracy:\n1. Every row of ERRORS.md (E1–E18): does the trigger exist in the C source? Is the \"expected C result\" column exactly what the C build does (verify at least 14 rows empirically by running a C build you make yourself in $TMPDIR)? Does each named test exist AND actually construct that condition (read the test bodies)? Are the greps quoted at the top reproducible verbatim?\n2. Every row of CONFIGS.md (C1–C33): do the named tests exist, and does each test body really cover the described configuration (counts, seeds, exhaustiveness, pipe-vs-file, tty, thread counts, byte sizes)? Flag any row whose description overstates what its test does.\n3. The \"Deliberately not emulated\" section of CONFIGS.md: verify each of the three claims and the quantitative claim (\"196/196 sequences that only call driver/main agree; 117 ordering-only; 46 host-stdio cases\"). Write your own dlopen host in $TMPDIR to check that pure sequences of driver/main calls really do agree, and that the three documented exceptions really are the only failures. Report if the numbers or the characterisation are wrong.\n4. src/driver_impl.rs: audit the new stream emulation for correctness against glibc semantics AND for latent bugs — the `C_STDIN` mutex state machine (buf/pos/len/pushback), the buffer-size rule (`st_blksize` capped at BUFSIZ=8192; is that what glibc does? check glibc's `_IO_file_doallocate` semantics as best you can and by measuring the C program's read sizes with strace if available), the `atexit` seek-back arithmetic (`(len - pos) + pushback`), the no-EINTR-retry choice, `CStdout`'s per-call locking, and any panic path (unwrap/expect/index/overflow) that could abort where C would not. Report concrete bug scenarios, ideally with a reproduction.\n5. Are there any *new* configuration axes introduced by the new design that CONFIGS.md does not have a row for (e.g. fd 0 pre-positioned with lseek, fd 0 O_APPEND, fd 0 with an unusual st_blksize, exit vs _exit vs abort, dlclose)? List them.\n6. Confirm the suite passes now: `timeout 600 cargo test --offline 2>&1 | tail -30`; report per-binary totals. Also confirm `Cargo.toml` still has no [features] and libloading in [dev-dependencies].\n7. Check for vacuous or self-fulfilling tests: any #[test] that asserts nothing meaningful, compares a value to itself, only checks lengths, catches its own failure, or would pass if the Rust `.so` were replaced by a copy of... anything. Also check no test calls Rust functions directly instead of through the dlopened `.so` exports.\n\nReport format: numbered findings marked [WRONG-CLAIM], [MISSING-COVERAGE], [VACUOUS-TEST], [BUG-RISK] or [OK-NOTE], each with file:line references and command+output evidence. Be concrete; no style opinions.","agentId":"a93b9e9a7fd43e2af","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Verdict\n\nThe suite is green (75/75 in every profile leg I ran) and the great majority of ERRORS.md / CONFIGS.md is now empirically correct — I independently re-verified **all 18 E-rows** against my own gcc build, the buffer-size rule, the seek-back arithmetic, the EINTR choice, the three \"not emulated\" rows and the 196/117/46 numbers. But the new stream emulation has **four real divergences from glibc that the artifacts claim do not exist**, three of which are reachable by a host that never touches glibc stdio — exactly the class CONFIGS.md declares empty.\n\nAll work done in `$TMPDIR/audit2/` (fresh `gcc`/`rustc` builds, my own dlopen host `h.c`). Nothing in the working dir was modified except `target/`.\n\n---\n\n### 1. [BUG-RISK] `main()` called concurrently from several threads corrupts converted values — C never does\n`src/driver_impl.rs:136` + `:373`: `CStdin::read` takes `C_STDIN` **per byte**, and `Scanner::peek/bump` release it between bytes. glibc's `scanf` holds `flockfile(stdin)` for the whole conversion, so two concurrent conversions can never split one digit run. Rust's can.\n\n```\n$ ./h ./libc.so R   < file(\"1234567890\")   # R = 4 threads x main()\nlibc.so    runs=60  UNEXPECTED_VALUES=0 torn=53 examples=[]\nlibrs.so   runs=60  UNEXPECTED_VALUES=6 torn=17 examples=[(234567890, 2), (1, 2), (136, 1), (2457890, 1)]\n```\n`234567890`, `2457890`, `136`, `1` are fragments of the single token `1234567890`. With four distinct tokens (120 runs): C = 0 complete lines with an unexpected value, Rust = 43 (e.g. `1222222222` = two tokens fused, `11111111` = a truncated token). C only ever produces *torn output lines* (byte interleaving, which C31 legitimately allows); Rust additionally produces wrong numbers.\n\n### 2. [BUG-RISK] Re-entering `main()` from a signal handler hangs forever\n`C_STDIN` (`src/driver_impl.rs:77`) is a plain `Mutex`; glibc's `flockfile` is recursive per thread. A `SA_RESTART` handler that calls the exported `main` while the outer `main` blocks in `read`:\n\n```\nreenter=main   libc.so    rc=0 out=b'07000000\\n00000000\\n' err=b'handler-enter\\nhandler-exit\\nouter-main-returned\\n'\nreenter=main   librs.so   *** HUNG (deadlock) *** out=b'' err=b'handler-enter\\n'\n```\n(`driver` from a handler is fine — Rust's stdout lock is reentrant; both printed `0df0ad0b\\n07000000\\n`.) A related exit-time deadlock exists: `c_stdin_atexit` (`:101`) locks `C_STDIN`, whereas glibc's `_IO_cleanup` deliberately runs lock-free.\n\n### 3. [BUG-RISK] + [WRONG-CLAIM] glibc's **sticky EOF** is not modelled; the code comment asserting the opposite is false\n`src/driver_impl.rs:217-219` says *\"glibc's EOF indicator is not consulted by `__uflow`, so a later conversion simply reads again\"*. Wrong: `_IO_new_file_underflow` begins with `if (fp->_flags & _IO_EOF_SEEN) return EOF;` (\"C99 requires EOF to be sticky\") and issues **no further `read`**. Two reproductions, both pure `main()` sequences:\n\n```\n# main(); <append \"456\" to the file via a separate fd>; main()\nlibc.so    7b000000 00000000      <- C never re-reads\nlibrs.so   7b000000 c8010000      <- Rust reads the appended 456\n\n# main() x4 (drains \"1 2 3\"), lseek(0,0,SEEK_SET), main()\nlibc.so    01000000 02000000 03000000 00000000 00000000\nlibrs.so   01000000 02000000 03000000 00000000 01000000\n```\nAlso means Rust issues extra `read(2)` calls after EOF on every repeated `main` (invisible to `c28_repeated_main_past_eof`, which only ever uses a static file).\n\n### 4. [BUG-RISK] `dlclose` fires the seek-back early, leaving fd 0 at a different offset\n`atexit` from a cdylib binds to that DSO's `__dso_handle`, so `__cxa_finalize` at `dlclose` runs `c_stdin_atexit` (`:101`, registered at `:121`). C's `_IO_cleanup` only runs at process exit.\n\n```\n$ ./h ./LIB 'm,C,T'  < \"1 2 3\"     # m=main, C=dlclose, T=lseek(0,0,SEEK_CUR)\nlibc.so    ret=0 tell=5 01000000\nlibrs.so   01000000 ret=0 tell=1     <- offset already rewound\n$ ./h ./LIB 'm,T'   (control)  -> both tell=5\n```\n\n### 5. [WRONG-CLAIM] CONFIGS.md:113-119 — the numbers reproduce, the characterisation does not\nThe counts are right: `pure.py`'s 14 m/d-only specs × 14 streams = **196, 0 mismatches** (reproduced), and `classify.py`'s 33 specs × 9 streams = 297 → **agree 134 / ordering-only 117 / other 46** (reproduced exactly; the 46 splits 26 stderr-only + 20 where the library's own stdout value differs, which the parenthetical does cover). But:\n* *\"in any order, any number of times, with any stream\"* is false — falsified by findings 1–4.\n* *\"The only divergences appear when the host itself uses glibc stdio in between\"* (`:115-116`) is false — findings 1–4 involve **no** host stdio.\n* All 196 cases use **pipe** stdin (`subprocess.run(input=…)`), single-threaded, ≤4 sequential calls. The regular-file / seek-back path is not in the 196 at all. I re-ran 30 m/d specs × 14 streams over **both** pipe and file stdin: 420/420 and 420/420 agree — so the conclusion survives for sequential single-threaded hosts, but the stated scope does not.\n\n### 6. [WRONG-CLAIM] CONFIGS.md:106-107 \"reachable **only** by a C host that shares glibc's own FILE objects … or by making an internal allocation fail\"\nFindings 1–4 are reachable without sharing any `FILE`. Also the `_exit`/`abort`/`fork` sub-case of row 1 needs no FILE sharing either — any host that `_exit`s. (Row 1/2/3 themselves all verified correct: `A01000000\\n` vs `01000000\\nA`; line lost on `_exit`(rc=0, empty)/`abort`(rc=134, empty) vs Rust emitting it; duplicated across `fork` (`01000000|01000000|` vs `01000000|`); `getchar(); main()` on `\"1 2 3\"` → C `02000000` vs Rust `00000000`; `( ulimit -v 24000; ./cexe ) < 16MiB-of-nines` → C `00000000`, Rust `ffffffff`, and both `ffffffff` unlimited.)\n\n### 7. [MISSING-COVERAGE] Configuration axes introduced by the new design with no CONFIGS.md row\nDiverging (should become rows/documented exceptions): concurrent `main()`; reentrant `main()`; `main()` after EOF on a re-seeked or grown descriptor; `dlclose`. Non-diverging but untested (I verified each agrees, so they are cheap rows): fd 0 with `st_blksize != 4096` (**pty slave = 1024 → both consume exactly 1024 bytes**), fd 0 pre-positioned with `lseek` (offsets 1 and 3), fd 0 `O_RDWR|O_APPEND`, fd 0 `O_WRONLY` (read→EBADF), fd 0 non-blocking empty pipe (EAGAIN), fd 0 = `/dev/null` / `/dev/zero` / a `/proc` file, fd 0 = tty (character-device *stdin*; C33 only covers tty stdout), `exit` vs `_exit` vs `abort` vs `fork` (documented in prose but no row and no test), `RTLD_*`/`dlclose`. Also untested: an 8 KiB buffer `malloc` failure — glibc falls back to a 1-byte unbuffered stream (`_IO_doallocbuf`), Rust's `vec![0u8; size]` (`:153`) would `handle_alloc_error`→abort. I could not open an observable RLIMIT_AS window for it, so treat as theoretical.\n\n### 8. [WRONG-CLAIM] CONFIGS.md:89 (C20) — \"unseekable stream, **short reads**\"\n`common/mod.rs:438-456` (`Stdin::Pipe`) writes all the data and closes the write end *before* the fork, so the child's first `read` returns everything (≤4096). No short read is ever produced. The only short/blocking-read wiring is `Stdin::SlowPipe`, used exclusively by E18.\n\n### 9. [WRONG-CLAIM] CONFIGS.md:99 (C31) — \"the ability to interleave **must** match\"\n`tests/stdin_stream.rs:297`: the interleaving assertion is gated on `if torn[0] > 0`, and even then only requires `torn[1] > 0`. There is no quantitative granularity comparison, and if C ever stopped tearing the row would assert nothing about interleaving. (It is not vacuous *here*: `torn lines over 3 rounds: C 2610 / Rust 2730`.)\n\n### 10. [VACUOUS-TEST] `e14_e15_print_hex_is_not_reachable_through_ffi` ignores `nm`'s exit status\n`tests/error_paths.rs:392-404`: `Command::new(\"nm\")…output().unwrap()` then `assert!(!text.contains(\"print_hex\"))`. If `nm` fails, `stdout` is empty and the assertion passes vacuously. `tests/symbols.rs:13-24` does check `out.status.success()` — apply the same there. Minor siblings: `tests/stdin_stream.rs:427` `assert!(f.as_raw_fd() > 2)` asserts nothing about either implementation; `tests/stdin_stream.rs:479-487` silently `return`s if `script(1)` is missing (present here: `util-linux 2.37.4`, so C33b did run).\n\n### 11. [OK-NOTE] Stale/mismatched test prose (no functional impact)\n`tests/main_valid.rs:174-175` still says C18 crosses \"Rust's 8 KiB `BufReader`\" — the design no longer uses a `BufReader`. `tests/driver_valid.rs:147-148` says `c24_driver_state_is_not_sticky` calls `driver` \"before and after a captured batch\"; the body has no batch and never calls `main`, so the \"no state leaks between entry points\" part of C24 rests solely on `c24_main_and_driver_interleaved`. ERRORS.md:56 \"All 18 rows have a passing **differential** test in `tests/error_paths.rs`\" — E14/E15's test is a symbol-absence check, and E18's two tests live in `stdin_stream.rs` (noted parenthetically). ERRORS.md:40 lists `-`+letter as an E4 trigger; that is E6's shape (covered by `e4b_all_byte_values_as_prefix`).\n\n### 12. [OK-NOTE] C27's `[x]` is not earned by `cargo test`\n`cargo test` runs dev-so + dev-cargo only. I ran three of the four legs: dev/dev, `RUST_SO_PROFILE=release` + dev cargo, and dev-so + `--release` cargo — **all 75 pass**. `scripts/verify_all.sh` does implement the full matrix (I read it, did not run it).\n\n---\n\n### Verified correct (item-by-item)\n\n* **Greps (ERRORS.md:9-14, CONFIGS.md:11)** reproduce verbatim: grep 1 → no matches (rc=1); grep 2 → exactly `24:`, `27:`, `41:`; `#if` count → 0; `wc -l` → 41.\n* **All 18 E-rows verified empirically** against my own `gcc 11.5.0 / glibc 2.34` build (requirement was ≥14): E1 `00000000`/0; E2 all six whitespace classes + mixes; E3 `0<&-` and `< .` → `00000000`/0; E4 all 12 listed bytes + the pushback claim (`\"x12\"`×2 → `00000000 00000000`; `\"a12 rest\"` leaves the descriptor at offset **0**); E5/E6 every listed case; E7 → `ffffffff` incl. 5000 nines; E8 → `00000000`; E9 all six values match the documented hex exactly; E10/E17 → 9 bytes + exit 0 for every input; E11 `>&-` exit 0; E12 `>/dev/full` exit 0; E13 signal 13 (raw wait `-13`); E14/E15 `nm -D` = `{driver, main}`, no `print_hex`; E16 wide args (`w-1,w4294967296,w9223372036854775807` → `ffffffff/00000000/ffffffff`, both libs); E18 non-restart → `00000000`, `SA_RESTART` → `07000000`, both libs. Prebuilt `c_src/build/driver` and `build_c/libdriver_c.so` behave as the table says.\n* **Buffer-size 