{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a56600b699e7fb319","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01RBZxyASGUZ53uMKCoS83gM","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wClUkV/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-wClUkV-translated-rust/64daaee6-dfd8-4ebc-87be-dd6361eb86bc/tasks/a56600b699e7fb319.output","summary":"All probes complete. Here is the report.\n\n---\n\n# CONFIRMED DIVERGENCES\n\nAll work done under `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wClUkV/tmp/h` (`$TMPDIR/h`); no file in the working dir was touched. `gcc 11.5.0`, `glibc 2.34`. Builds: `$TMPDIR/h/c_exe`, `$TMPDIR/h/libc.so`, `$TMPDIR/h/librs.so`, `target/debug/driver`, `target/release/driver`.\n\n## 1. glibc's *sticky* EOF indicator is not modelled — a later `main()` converts data the C build never sees (WRONG VALUE + WRONG BYTES CONSUMED)\n\n`src/driver_impl.rs:213-221` (`Scanner::new_stdin`) states *\"glibc's EOF indicator is not consulted by `__uflow`, so a later conversion simply reads again\"*. That is false for glibc: `_IO_new_file_underflow` begins with `if (fp->_flags & _IO_EOF_SEEN) return EOF;` (\"C99 requires EOF to be sticky\"). So once a conversion has hit EOF, **C issues no further `read` on fd 0 for the rest of the process**, while the translation reads again. Whenever new data has become available after that EOF, Rust converts and prints it and consumes it from the descriptor; C does not.\n\n### Repro 1a — growing regular file (self-contained, 2 commands)\n```\ncd $TMPDIR/h && gcc -O0 -o repro_eof repro_eof.c -ldl\ntimeout 30 ./repro_eof $TMPDIR/h/libc.so ; timeout 30 ./repro_eof $TMPDIR/h/librs.so\n```\n`repro_eof.c` = dlopen host: fd 0 = `eof.bin` (O_RDONLY), call exported `main()`, append `\" 77\"`, call `main()` again, `exit(0)`.\nInput: file `\\x34\\x32` (`\"42\"`), then appended `\\x20\\x37\\x37` (`\" 77\"`).\n\n| | C (`libc.so`) | Rust (`librs.so`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| stdout | `2a000000\\n00000000\\n` | `2a000000\\n4d000000\\n` |\n| fd 0 final offset | `2` | `5` |\n| left for next reader | `\" 77\"` | `\"\"` |\n\nSame via `$TMPDIR/h/host` (`grow:` op), 75 of 168 `main,grow:<x>,main` shapes diverge (`$TMPDIR/h/quantify.py`). E.g. empty file + `\"9\"`: C `00000000\\n00000000\\n`, Rust `00000000\\n09000000\\n`.\n\n### Repro 1b — real terminal: EOF (VEOF) followed by more input\n`$TMPDIR/h/sticky.py` section C. pty in canonical mode, master writes `\\x34\\x32\\x0a` `\\x04` `\\x37\\x37\\x0a`; slave = fd 0; host script `main,main,main`.\n* C: `2a000000\\n00000000\\n00000000\\n`\n* Rust: `2a000000\\n00000000\\n4d000000\\n`\n\n### Repro 1c — host lseeks fd 0 (80 of 224 shapes diverge)\n```\ncd $TMPDIR/h && printf '42' > g1 && gcc -O0 -o host2 host2.c -ldl\ntimeout 20 ./host2 $TMPDIR/h/libc.so  \"main,seek0,main\" < g1\ntimeout 20 ./host2 $TMPDIR/h/librs.so \"main,seek0,main\" < g1\n```\nInput `\\x34\\x32`. C stdout `2a000000\\n00000000\\n`, fd 0 offset 0; Rust `2a000000\\n2a000000\\n`, offset 2.\n\n### Mechanism proof\n`./host2 <so> \"main,feof,clearerr,feof,seek0,main\" < g1`: C reports `[feof=1]`, and with `clearerr(stdin)` inserted C becomes byte-identical to Rust (`2a0000002a000000`). Independently, the ptrace tracer (`$TMPDIR/h/tracer.c`) shows **48 of 182** traced sequences differ, always by Rust emitting one extra `read(fd=0, 4096) = 0` per subsequent `main()` that C never issues, e.g. input `\\x31`×4096, script `main,main`:\n* C: `read=4096`, `read=0`\n* Rust: `read=4096`, `read=0`, `read=0`\n\nNot one of the three documented exclusions: no host stdio is involved, and CONFIGS.md explicitly claims the sticky EOF indicator (row C28) and descriptor-level consumption (row C30) are matched.\n\n## 2. `dlclose` runs the exit-time seek-back early\n\nThe `atexit` registration in `install_atexit_hook` (`src/driver_impl.rs:110-123`) resolves to `libc_nonshared.a`'s `atexit`, i.e. `__cxa_atexit(..., &__dso_handle)` of `librs.so`, so `__cxa_finalize` runs the hook **at `dlclose` time**. glibc's `_IO_cleanup` never runs at `dlclose` (libc stays mapped), so C rewinds only at process exit.\n\n```\ncd $TMPDIR/h && printf '  1234xyz rest-of-file\\n' > t1\ntimeout 20 ./host2 $TMPDIR/h/libc.so  \"main,off,dlclose,off,readfd0\" < t1\ntimeout 20 ./host2 $TMPDIR/h/librs.so \"main,off,dlclose,off,readfd0\" < t1\n```\nInput `\\x20\\x20\\x31\\x32\\x33\\x34\\x78\\x79\\x7a\\x20\\x72\\x65\\x73\\x74\\x2d\\x6f\\x66\\x2d\\x66\\x69\\x6c\\x65\\x0a`\n\n* C: `[off=23][dlclose=0][off=23][read0=0:]` — the host reads nothing after `dlclose`.\n* Rust: `[off=23][dlclose=0][off=6][read0=16:xyz rest-of-file]` — the host reads back 16 bytes the C program keeps hidden until exit.\n\n(13 of the `main,off,dlclose,off` groups in `$TMPDIR/h/hostcmp.py` diverge. The *final* offset after exit agrees; only the timing differs, and it is observable by the host in between.)\n\n## 3. The seek-back runs *before* host `atexit` handlers registered before the first call\n\nglibc runs `_IO_cleanup` from the `__libc_atexit` hook, i.e. after **all** `atexit` handlers. The translation's hook is an ordinary `atexit` handler registered lazily during the first conversion, so LIFO order puts it *first*.\n\n```\ntimeout 20 ./host2 $TMPDIR/h/libc.so  \"atexitA,main\" < t1\ntimeout 20 ./host2 $TMPDIR/h/librs.so \"atexitA,main\" < t1\n```\nInput as above. Host's `atexit` handler prints `lseek(0,0,SEEK_CUR)`.\n* C: `[hostatexitA:off=23]`\n* Rust: `[hostatexitA:off=6]`\n\n`\"main,atexitA\"` agrees (`off=23` both), which isolates the cause to registration order.\n\n## 4. The Rust binary clobbers an inherited `SIGPIPE = SIG_IGN`\n\n`src/main.rs:16-25` unconditionally sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_DFL`. `SIG_IGN` survives `exec`, so a parent that ignores SIGPIPE (CPython does this, as do many daemons) gets a different outcome.\n\n```\ncd $TMPDIR/h && gcc -O0 -o sigpipe sigpipe.c && printf '  1234xyz rest-of-file\\n' > t1\ntimeout 20 ./sigpipe $TMPDIR/h/c_exe ign t1\ntimeout 20 ./sigpipe .../target/debug/driver   ign t1\ntimeout 20 ./sigpipe .../target/release/driver ign t1\n```\n(parent: `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_IGN)`, child stdout = pipe with no reader, stdin = t1)\n\n| | C | Rust debug | Rust release |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| status | `exit=0` | `killed_by_signal=13` | `killed_by_signal=13` |\n| fd 0 final offset | `6` (seek-back done) | `23` (no seek-back) | `23` |\n\nWith `SIGPIPE = SIG_DFL` in the parent, all three agree (`killed_by_signal=13`, offset 23) — so the patch is right only for the default case.\n\n## 5. The conversion is not atomic: concurrent `main()` callers split a number\n\nglibc's `__vfscanf_internal` holds the `stdin` FILE lock for the whole conversion. The translation takes `C_STDIN` per *byte* (`CStdin::read`), so two threads can each take one digit of the same token. CONFIGS row C31 requires the byte multiset to match.\n\n```\ncd $TMPDIR/h && gcc -O0 -o threads threads.c -ldl -lpthread\npython3 threadcmp2.py     # tokens are all \"42\"; the record for 42 is \"2a000000\" (no '4')\n```\nInput: `\\x34\\x32\\x20` × 450, 4/8/16 threads × (400/nthreads) `main()` calls.\n\n* C: **20/20 trials** → exactly `\"4\"=0, \"a\"=400` (400 intact `2a000000` records, 3600 bytes).\n* Rust: **18/20 trials at ≥4 threads** → `\"4\"` appears and `\"a\"` drops, e.g. nt=8: `\"4\"=8, \"a\"=383` — a `\"42\"` token was split into records for `4` (`04000000`) and `2` (`02000000`). Byte count (3600) and record count (400) still match; the multiset does not.\n\nAt 2 threads both were clean in 5/5 trials. `driver`-only concurrency (4 threads × 300 calls) matched exactly in 3/3 trials.\n\n## 6. fd 0 == fd 1 + repeated `main()`: the library overwrites its own input\n\nDirect consequence of the documented stdout-flush-timing difference (table row 1), but the effect lands on the *input* stream and final file content, not merely on ordering, so flagging it explicitly.\n\nfd 0 and fd 1 are one `O_RDWR` descriptor on a file; host script `main,main`. Input `\\x31` + `\\x20`×4200 + `\\x32\\x20\\x33\\x20\\x34\\x0a` (4207 bytes).\n\n| | C | Rust |\n|---|---|---|\n| file size after | 4225 | 4216 |\n| bytes[4090:4104] | all spaces | `01000000\\n` written *into the input* |\n| tail | `…2 3 4\\n01000000\\n02000000\\n` | `…2 3 4\\n02000000\\n` |\n| fd 0 final offset | 4220 | 4211 |\n\nSingle-shot (`./driver <> file`) agrees for all 5 payloads tested, including 4090-space and 12x34 shapes.\n\n---\n\n# Probed and found IDENTICAL\n\n* **Parser fuzz** — 4980 inputs (`$TMPDIR/h/fuzz1.py`): 2^31/2^32/2^63/2^64/10^40 boundaries, `LONG_MAX/MIN±1`, signs, `++`/`+-`, 40 leading zeros, all 6 C whitespace kinds plus `\\x1c-\\x1f`/`\\x85`, NUL, high-bit bytes, `0x`/`1e5`/`1_000`/`1'000`, junk tails, interesting byte exactly at offsets 4094-4097 / 8190-8193, 800 random 1-45-digit signed numbers, 4000 random alphabet strings. Compared stdout **and** exit status **and** fd 0's final offset, with stdin a file and a pipe, against C and **both** cargo profiles → **0 diffs**.\n* **fd 0 shapes** — 49 groups (`fd0.py`): regular file, `O_RDWR`, `O_RDWR|O_APPEND`, pre-`lseek` to 3 / to EOF / past EOF, empty file, via a symlink, `/dev/shm` (tmpfs), `/proc/self/stat`, `/proc/uptime`, `/proc/self/cmdline`, `/proc/self/maps` (st_blksize 1024 — buffer sized identically), `/dev/zero`, `/dev/null`, `/dev/full`, a **directory** fd, a **write-only** fd, **closed** fd 0, pipe (writer closed and writer still open), FIFO, socketpair, payloads 0-9001 bytes. Compared stdout, exit status, final offset and the bytes left for a co-reader → **0 diffs** (2 groups differed only in nondeterministic `/proc` content).\n* **Syscall sequences on fd 0** (ptrace, `tracer.c` + `tracecmp.py`) — 26 inputs × executables and 6 dlopen scripts, 182 comparisons: **read sizes/counts and every `lseek` are identical** except the sticky-EOF extra `read()=0` of divergence 1. Single-run executables: **0/26 diffs**. The only other syscall differences are `fstat` vs `statx` for fd 0 and C's extra `fstat(fd=1)` (stdout buffer sizing) — both stdout-buffering related.\n* **Repeated `main()` without post-EOF data** — 2568 comparisons (`final.py`): 214 random/boundary token streams × `main`×1/2/3/5 and interleaved `driver`, over file and pipe stdin, comparing stdout, host markers, exit status, final offset and leftovers → **0 diffs**.\n* **dlopen host sequences** — 430 groups (`hostcmp.py`): `main`×1-5, `driver`+`main` interleavings, `dlclose`, `dlclose,reopen,main`, `fork`, 16 input shapes over file and pipe stdin. Everything agreed except items 1-3 above and the documented `_exit`/`abort`/`SIGTERM`/`SIGSEGV`/`fork` stdout-drop family (85 groups — **this is documented table row 1**, \"a host that `_exit`s/`abort`s/`fork`s after the call sees the buffered line dropped/duplicated\"; `SIGTERM`/`SIGSEGV` are the same mechanism).\n* **A host that dlopens BOTH .so files** — loading the other library as an unused spectator perturbs neither, in both directions, for `main`, `main,main` and `driver:7,main` (identical stdout, host markers, final offset).\n* **stdout sinks** — `/dev/full`, `/dev/null`, closed fd 1, `O_APPEND` fd 1 with pre-existing content, a real pty as stdout, fd0==fd1 single-shot: identical exit status, fd 0 offset and file content.\n* **stdin is a real pty** (single run, 4 inputs): identical stdout, status and leftover on the master.\n* **Locale** — 162 groups: `LC_ALL`/`LANG`/`LC_NUMERIC` ∈ {C, POSIX, en_US.UTF-8, tr_TR.UTF-8, de_DE.UTF-8, fr_FR.UTF-8, C.UTF-8, ru_RU.KOI8-R, invalid} × 6 inputs → **0 diffs**.\n* **Resource limits** — `RLIMIT_FSIZE=1`, `RLIMIT_AS=64 MiB`, `RLIMIT_STACK=256 KiB` × 6 inputs → identical. `RLIMIT_NOFILE` 1-5 → both fail to exec with status 127 (only the `ld.so` message names a different missing library: `libc.so.6` vs `libgcc_s.so.1`); at 8 both work identically.\n* **EINTR** — host installs a `SIGALRM` handler without `SA_RESTART`, `alarm(1)`, fd 0 = a blocking empty pipe: both fail the conversion and print `00000000`, for one and two `main()` calls. Only difference: `errno` as seen by the host after a *second* call (C 0, Rust 4/EINTR) — glibc's internal errno bookkeeping, no effect on stdout/status/descriptor.\n* **`posix_spawn` vs `fork`+`exec`** — both used across the harnesses, no difference.\n\nNo divergence found in `print_hex`/`driver` byte formatting, in buffer sizing (`st_blksize` capping at `BUFSIZ` matches `_IO_file_doallocate` exactly, verified on 1024- and 4096-blocksize fds), in the `ungetc` pushback (`\"12x34\"`, `\"12-34\"`, `\"12 34\"` all identical including the exit-time seek-back), or in any `lseek` amount.","usage":{"total_tokens":171868,"tool_uses":64,"duration_ms":1569998},"uuid":"5f5a3c3f-e788-41dc-92b4-ace2048b765a","session_id":"64daaee6-dfd8-4ebc-87be-dd6361eb86bc"}
